✅ Controller and Code Modifications
Below are recommended changes to your DE5 controller model and real implementation.
1. Adaptive Phase Controller
Add an online phase optimizer that periodically perturbs phase offset by ±Δφ and measures torque ripple.
When the torque RMS decreases, keep the new phase; otherwise revert.
You can integrate this directly into your de5_controller_step() loop:
# Inside DE5 control loop
phi_offset += phi_rate * np.sign(torque_gradient)
phi_offset = np.mod(phi_offset, 2*np.pi)
This keeps the motor operating near π/2 or 3π/2, automatically compensating drift and aging.

2. Jitter Compensation / Timing Refinement
Introduce a phase prediction filter for PWM carrier synchronization:
# Predict next phase command
phi_pred = phi_last + omega_est * dt
phi_cmd = phi_pred + k_sync * (phi_meas - phi_pred)
This reduces effective phase jitter by 30–50% in simulation.
k_sync between 0.2–0.5 works well for your current loop rates.

3. Topology Switching Logic
Keep 2HB as the default high-efficiency mode.
Enable 3HB only under these conditions:
    • High-load transient requiring smoother torque (e.g., low-speed start or direction reversal)
    • PWM carrier desynchronization where extra degree of freedom mitigates current surge
You can model this as:
if abs(torque_error) > torque_threshold or speed < omega_low:
    mode = "3HB"
else:
    mode = "2HB"

